          <div class="lesson-section-title">Brain Science: Breathing Notes</div>
          <span class="lesson-section-toggle">▾</span>
        </div>
        <div class="lesson-section-body">
          <p><strong>Nose breathing:</strong> Inhaling through the nose supports calm, focused awareness. Inhalations should equal exhalations for balance.</p>
          <br>
          <p><strong>To activate calm (parasympathetic system):</strong> Extend the exhale so it's <em>longer</em> than the inhale. Try: breathing out through nose, humming, hissing, or saying "shhh."</p>
          <br>
          <p><strong>What's happening in the brain:</strong> Diaphragmatic (belly) breathing reduces cortisol, releases acetylcholine (calming), and through neuroplasticity, strengthens neural pathways for reflective rather than reactive responses.</p>
          <div class="tip-box">
            <strong>🎯 Key for kids</strong>
            "Notice your belly rise and fall" — this anchors attention to a concrete physical sensation, making it easier than abstract breath focus.
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

